.); United States. Army. Corps of Engineers; Geotechnical Laboratory (U.S.)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"135145446":{"pageid":135145446,"ns":6,"title":"File:World elevation map.png","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":75282262,"width":21600,"height":10800,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/World_elevation_map.png/1920px-World_elevation_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":900,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/World_elevation_map.png/3840px-World_elevation_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/World_elevation_map.png/1920px-World_elevation_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":900,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/World_elevation_map.png/3840px-World_elevation_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/World_elevation_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:World_elevation_map.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=135145446","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"This is an height map of the planet earth at approximately 1.8km per pixel (at the equator). It uses an Equirectangular projection, so it's well suited for usage in mapping software. \n<p>It was done by combining two images from Nasa's Blue Marble collection, the [Topography](<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/images/73934/topography\">https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/images/73934/topography</a>) and [Bathymetry](<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/images/73963/bathymetry\">https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/images/73963/bathymetry</a>) maps. These images were made by Jesse Allen, NASA's Earth Observatory, using data from the General Bathymetric Chart of the Oceans (GEBCO) produced by the British Oceanographic Data Centre. These images are, according to their Image use policy [freely available for re-publication or re-use, including commercial purposes](<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/image-use-policy\">https://visibleearth.nasa.gov/image-use-policy</a>).\n</p>\nBoth images were combined over a gray background. The gray is #8f8f8f which was chosen for having a brightness of 56% which is the ration between of sea level between added height the Mt Everest and Mariana Trench. Bathymetry images were added as a Multiply blend, meaning it's subtracted from the sea level and Topography using a Screen Blend meaning it's added.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2023-07-28","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Avsa\" title=\"User:Avsa\">Avsa</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]}}}}